+*   **Limitations**: Requires explicit connectivity information. Degenerate cells must be handled explicitly. Performance may degrade for highly complex or concave geometries due to multiple re-entries.
 
 ## Background
-This method was introduced by [S1] as a technique for raytracing irregular volume data. It addresses the limitations of regular grid traversal when applied to unstructured meshes [S1]. The approach relies on the geometric properties of the cells (tetrahedra or hexahedra) and their adjacency relationships [S1]. For hexahedra, if symmetric tessellation is not possible, the method suggests subdividing each hexahedron into twenty-four tetrahedra by adding a vertex at the center of each face [S1].
+This method was introduced in the paper "Raytracing irregular volume data" [S1]. It addresses the need for rendering techniques that can handle non-uniform volumetric data, such as those arising from finite element analysis or geological modeling, where regular grid structures are not suitable.
 
